k that's a good question the  electorate is probably going to look a  little bit different  one of the things in talking to people  on the ground in georgia they'll say  that stacey abrams and her folks the way  they organized  was really service oriented and i'd be  curious to see whether they can take  that same approach for this  runoff election which is again 45 days  after the general election and  kind of in the middle of a really  interesting political season  for georgians stacey abrams has really  been viewed on democrats  among democrats as kind of the mvp of  this cycle for putting georgia in play  not just in this runoff but really for  helping joe biden deliver the state  two months ago what do you mean by  service oriented  well that's interesting right so going  and organizing people  around what their needs are helping  people get unemployment checks or  helping people connect to social  services  and using that to register voters that's  actually something that democrats have  used in the state  to be effective also it's not just  african americans i know that that's a  niche issue people like to talk about  it's also asian americans and latinos in  georgia  are really surging populations and  abrams and her organizers have really  taken advantage of that as well  so true so president trump has of course  made unfounded attacks against georgian  election officials claiming that that  election was was rigged essentially  how do you think that might affect his  supporters in terms of  them even coming out to the polls do  they still trust the process  that's a good question i know that  georgia republican officials have to be  concerned about that i mean  essentially what president trump is  asking georgia republicans to believe  is that his election was uh was was  corrupt in some way but all these other  state officials  in georgia were in on it that brian kemp  and the secretary of state were all  republicans who voted for  donald trump that they worked with  democrats to steal the election from him  that's a tall tale to tell the the the  backside of that is  whether georgians will actually trust  the process georgia georgia voters who  like donald trump are they going to  trust the process so you have this  interesting dynamic here of where you  need donald trump to heat up the base if  you're a republican but you're also  concerned about him  depressing excitement and depressing  enthusiasm so joel tell us are we in for  another election week or should we know  the results on election night  well i would put it like this if we know  what happens on election night my census  is probably a good night for republicans  because it's probably an easy  victory for purdue and lofler if it  takes a little bit longer that's  probably better for democrats because  that means it's a closer election and  really democrats best chance here is if  it's a close election that they can eke  out  speaking of purdue and lafleur what do  you think are their chances  um you know how will the fact that they  support the two thousand dollar stimulus  check  how do you think that'll affect them  quickly because we don't have a ton of  time  they did what they needed to do i think  what's interesting is how this  this race has really got shrouded in  race kelly loffler  has been campaigning with someone who is  purported to be a part of the ku klux  klan  she's been making attacks on rafael  warnock so that has  really become kind of a central issue in  the race over the last  72 hours or so we'll see if that carries  it to election day  joel payne thank you
 

